tor Is Necessary" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050950 :topic/math-academy-id 3930 :topic/title "Finite Population Corrections for Sample Means"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ced5604-9ef2-5ab7-b9a6-0f85058e1b91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187467} {:answer/id #uuid "94ffdbd1-5606-51d8-880d-2ce13edb6dc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187468} {:answer/id #uuid "b0a712f2-1df4-5b1f-afe2-ac43c3a8298a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187469} {:answer/id #uuid "f59830f8-e14e-5d81-8e91-2614d7c72ed8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187470} {:answer/id #uuid "2b880498-c8c5-537a-ac51-75dd3342bfdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187471}] :answer-field/id #uuid "c27a416f-832a-5692-b05b-f1e7e7b6119c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187466}] :question/id #uuid "6aae3317-3bff-5f38-a9d5-5047a92f18b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-226010" :question/problem "Which of the following situations is it necessary to use a finite population correction factor to adjust the variance in the distribution of the sample mean?\n\n1. A sample of size $n = 200$ from a population of size $N = 1000$ with population mean $μ = 1$ and population variance $σ^{2} = 4$.\n2. A sample of size $n = 100$ from a population of size $N = 10000$ with population mean $μ = 2$ and population variance $σ^{2} = 1$.\n3. A sample of size $n = 30$ from a population of size $N = 1000$ with population mean $μ = 3$ and population variance $σ^{2} = 25$.\n\n*Hint. You may assume all samples are conducted without replacement.*"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188911 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188273 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e12609b7-286b-50a8-8373-a331d60ab904" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Rank-Nullity Theorem" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049880 :topic/math-academy-id 3852 :topic/title "The Rank-Nullity Theorem in Terms of Linear Transformations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b15598d0-a300-5067-80a0-4f58a33bfcfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187543} {:answer/id #uuid "c3f5f5cd-2725-5b56-bf55-6d1ac9c5771b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187544} {:answer/id #uuid "1a629bb9-2d45-5781-b023-9d727557486c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187545} {:answer/id #uuid "24104593-8a31-518d-b3ab-dedc0c1ac151" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187546} {:answer/id #uuid "36af65ed-cc42-5b73-9282-fda21d2e073e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187547}] :answer-field/id #uuid "ffcaf15a-b093-5793-9500-03e1ad502daa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187542} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80413058-7a42-50ce-b340-c38037f5e452" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366034} {:answer/id #uuid "88e1d287-8927-58d8-8cc3-0f5341148e02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366035} {:answer/id #uuid "56750f1c-d37b-5ed0-95b2-543990047e30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366036} {:answer/id #uuid "e9c88594-75cf-5584-aa94-856988c0b499" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366037} {:answer/id #uuid "36e38e8d-e602-5755-9e6f-06535d1903bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366038}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36e38e8d-e602-5755-9e6f-06535d1903bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366038} :answer-field/id #uuid "609eb989-9967-575f-a39a-2755b35e41be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366033}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e86ac40c-9f96-593f-88c6-aef65bbc3b17" :question/math-academy-id "q-38136" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{5}\\to {R}^{5}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and let ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ be a set of linearly independent vectors in ${R}^{5}.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n*If  $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$, then $\\text{__________}.$ *\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\n- $\\text{rank}(T)=1$\n- $\\text{nullity}(T)=2$\n- $T$ is a bijection" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{5}$ into ${R}^{5},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=5 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=5.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ and the set ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent, we get that $\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=3.$\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =5-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =5-3 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 5$ (or since $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0$), the linear transformation $T$ cannot be a bijection according to the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, the correct option is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188941 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188308 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1cb54962-c70f-5019-85ce-82f4b577c1dd" :knowledge-point/title "Comparing Whole Numbers on a Number Line" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050946 :topic/math-academy-id 3919 :topic/title "Comparing Whole Numbers Using Place Value"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aeabe59c-34e4-58fd-ac15-971fdca22700" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187723} {:answer/id #uuid "f32c558a-3251-5a52-8f54-5d52aec69513" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187724} {:answer/id #uuid "094e36c1-9bde-571f-936b-6047567bba5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592